ay also called ESI quadrupole  mass spectrometry is you usually  integrated with inline with some sort of  liquid chromatography and I depict the  process in the bottom right where a hplc  is spraying its contents into it through  a nebulizer into a quadrupole mass  spectrometer and in this case the charge  state of the proteins or peptides is  related to the number of positively  charged amino acids that is arginine and  lysine and it's usually conducted under  very low pH conditions so that all of  the carboxylates are at that point  neutral and then one can if especially  through if you have multiple quadruples  connected in tandem you can fragment the  peptides along the mi backbone and then  read out the amino acid content of the  peptides like I show in the ESI  quadrupole spectra at the top right  where the distance between each of the  peaks is typically  a function of the peptides that are  fragmented along the backbone and used  in an automated way the instruments will  read out the spectra and compare the  spectra to spectra in a database in  order to confirm the identity of the  peptides that you are working with or  like oscillation patterns as well so in  phase three I've talked a bit about the  panning for binders so in an earlier  publication as a postdoc I wished to  uncover the connectivity of proteins and  signaling networks and through this  exercise we cloned and expressed  hundreds of Sark amala g two domains  these are the proteins that recognize  activated growth factor receptors at the  time that i started the postdoc many of  much of the genome had still not yet  been sequenced very well and many of the  proteins in the genome still the  function wasn't clear so we cloned all  of the so-called starcom ology two  domains which have a certain motif that  is predicted it was predicted at the  time to recognize phosphotyrosine motifs  have growth back to receptors and we  wanted to to query the interaction  potential of all of the epidermal growth  factor receptors with all of these  signaling proteins downstream that we  knew were most likely responsible for  the cancer signal of epidermal growth  factor receptor so in this method we use  protein microarrays in this method we  took the collections of the hundreds of  sh2 domains and we arrayed them on to  the surfaces of glass slides and using  this method you're able to screen the  interaction potential of hundreds of  proteins that are  on the slide simultaneously with  hundreds of other fluorescently labeled  proteins or peptides  okay my screen okay here we go so in  this context we actually combined the  previous technology of microscope slide  arrays with the microtiter plate  technology that's often used to to  automate through liquid handling robots  and what I'm showing here on the right  is this merging of protein microarrays  of microtiter technology where each of  the microarrays is contained within the  well of a microtiter plate so that a  mated pipetting robot can then be used  to add different peptides for poor  screening purposes and what I show and  depicting here is a small amount of  fluorescent dye that has been doped into  each of the proteins that we arrayed  onto the slide and when we incubated our  slide with with phosphopeptides that  were derived from the growth factor  receptor then we could start to see that  there were different proteins on the  slide that bound in different affinities  to the peptide that was incubated and  when we used a saturation binding curve  we could fit affinities there were some  peptides that maximize their signal at  very low concentration such as the able  to kinase that bound to this particular  protein of her to also known as herb be  too and the specific phosphorylation  site here was tyrosine 1139 but we could  also characterize if we incubated at  very high concentrations of the peptide  we would see a saturation with lower  affinity proteins such as the breast  cancer anti-estrogen resistance three  protein we had to it the midpoint of  this binding curve was one micromolar  vs.  71 nanomolar for the able to kinase but  because this was all done in an  automated manner in terms of the micro  fluidics or sorry not the micro fluidics  but in terms of the liquid hand length  and in terms of the data extraction via  the image analysis we are able to  quickly characterize the binding  potentials of all 150 of the putative  phospho tyrosine binding proteins in the  in the human genome and we used these  affinity maps to characterize for each  growth factor receptor the epidermal  growth factor receptor also known as rp1  in the top left or b2 in the top right  which is today typically known as her to  her b3 at the bottom left really  recruited the phosphoinositide kinases  really well those were all depicted in  red and then or before which wasn't well  understood at all and I believe today as  it's still a little bit not well  characterized but it's thought to play a  role in in the liver is characterized at  the bottom right so in panning for  binders arrays can be used also the  enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay that I  described before can be used to pan for  binders it's simply with the Eliza  approach one typically is characterizing  96 interactions on a slide versus with  the micro-ray method one can  characterize thousands of interactions  on a slide using thousands of fold less  reagents so finally I will talk about  biophysical and biochemical  characterization methods it's often  times as i showed in these previous few  slides when you're in performing any  particular study you will lean quite  heavily  one particular method or one particular  instrument and it's very important in  order to be a very well-rounded  biochemist biophysicist to have at least  some grounding in a variety of methods  so that when one sees a particular  problem one can quickly identify a  series of potential solutions in order  to move forward in your research so in  the first method I will talk about for  biophysical characterization is light  scattering so the Delta max core is an  example of an instrument that Beckman  Coulter cells which uses both dynamic  and static light scattering to quickly  characterize the molecular weight and  the size distribution and get an  indication of the aggregation state of  proteins in the solution phase without  any label and with light scattering the  correlation of scatter and time will  provide an indication of the sizes of  the proteins in solution so I show at  the top right an example of the large  protein or it could be a protein or it  could be other types of molecules that  the idea is when light is scattering in  time it is quite correlated if the  larger the protein is because it's  moving relatively slowly as the protein  if it is smaller it will actually be  moving much quicker by Brownian motion  and the change in scatter scattering  patterns in time will change much  quicker and using the algorithms of the  instrument it will in the matter of  seconds with a very small amount of  material as few as a few microliters one  can quickly obtain the molecular weight  and the size distribution and the  polydispersity or the range of sizes of  proteins in solution and quickly gain an  understanding of of the quality of  protein that you're working with the  delsym x-pro is an example of an  instrument that uses both  light-scattering as well as a voltage  potential application in order to derive  a charge from protein at the point at  which formulations are developed often  the charge of the proteins and solution  is critical in order for determining  whether or not the proteins will be  stable in solution because of their  repulsion for each other or whether or  not they will tend to coalesce and  agglomerate and fall out of solution in  many types of industries for example in  in water quality they're trying to find  conditions in which the the particles  are neutral so that they will coalesce  and come out of solution during the  water purification process so with a  particular Delta max pro instrument it  has a couple of interesting modules that  are very important in the context of  proteins the first is the so-called  assist module so typically as you're  applying a potential to a solution that  has salt there will be bubbles generated  due to the electric current that will  will generate bubbles and because  bubbles scatter light they interfere  with your ability to collect zeta  potential measurements and with the  assist it actually pressurizes the the  flow cell so that the bubbles don't form  and therefore you can actually measure  the zeta potential or the charge state  of the particles in which in cases where  you typically can't the other  interesting feature of the delsym x-pro  is that it has 32 detectors at different  angles which collect scattering  information in parallel because proteins  are very sensitive if with a standard  zeta potential measuring instrument  that's not been  designed for proteins you will typically  require several minutes in order to  collect enough light scattering data  under the conditions of alternating  electric current before you can gather  enough information with which to derive  a zeta potential whereas with the Delta  maxpro it collects the light scattering  information in parallel for many  detector simultaneously allowing you to  collect the information within a few  seconds and therefore be able to collect  the information before the protein is  destroyed or or degraded due to the  application of the electric potential so  by off during the biophysical and  biochemical characterization process  surface plasmon resonance is also often  used in order to measure in a label-free  manner the binding of and the affinity  actually of an immobilized protein on a  gold surface with proteins that are  flowing in solution and so the first  protein is typically immobilized and  then a second protein small molecule  peptide is flown through a microfluidic  chamber and as more mass binds to the  surface it creates a change in signal  that can be measured by the detector and  what one is typically then examining is  a change from a base level response on  the surface to a maximal level of  response as a function of concentration  of the analyte that's in solution and  through this method one can determine on  rates off rates and steady state binding  profiles  recently a surface acoustic wave  technology has been used to generate  slightly more information than the  surface plasmon resonance method and  through this method if you look at the  binding profiles in the that I show in  the top right what you see our curves  that look very similar to spr however  with the surface acoustic wave  technology using both of the phase of  the acoustic wave on the surface as well  as the amplitude one can examine not  only changes in binding but also changes  in the confirmation of the protein on  the cell surface and again this is this  you can perform kinetic analysis and you  can perform this in a label-free manner  microscale thermophoresis is yet another  way to measure protein interaction  potentials or small molecules peptides  this does use fluorescence so it's it's  there but there are two different  flavors that this can be performed in  one manner intrinsic tryptophan  fluorescence can be used to read out  protein fluorescence and the other you  can attach a flu or to the protein of  interest but in any event with this  method a laser is used to create a  temperature gradient and depending upon  the binding state of your protein the  larger is the slower that it will  migrate along the temperature gradient  and therefore if one performs a  titration series from low low protein to  high protein with a constant amount of  the fluorescently labeled version one  can then quantify a  saturation binding curve as I show in  the bottom right whereas ligand  concentration goes up the the speed at  which the protein is migrating out of  the thermal faradic gradient goes down  so another flavor of this interaction  analysis is bio layer interferometry  with this method the proteins are  actually immobilized on the surface of a  tip and a interference pattern along the  tip is measured as proteins are binding  to the tip and so this tip is typically  dipped into 96 or 384-well plates and  the interference pattern along the tip  is then examined and if you look at the  D sensor gram at the bottom right you'll  see a graph that looks very similar to  spr and the other methods that i've  shown you go from a base state where  nothing is bound to the tip as you begin  to dip into solutions that end up  binding to the surface of your tip with  its immobilized proteins you'll start to  see an increase in the intensity of the  signal in a relationship to the affinity  of your analyte for your proteins this  is again label-free and because it can  be done in 96 or 384-well plate sits  quite high throughput another important  tool is multi angle light scattering so  with multiple angle light scattering  this is typically performed downstream  of size exclusion chromatography as a  way to get an additional corroboration  of the size of a protein so typically  the  the spectrograph or Spectre chromatogram  rather that one takes off of the size  exclusion chromatography is overlaid  with a series of sizing measurements  taken during the online run and in many  cases the protein shape will make it  perform aberrantly on the size exclusion  chromatography and vice versa and the  multiple angles gathering downstream  will allow one to have a second added  level of confidence that the size  readings obtained from the chromatogram  is consistent and in cases where they're  not consistent one can dig deeper to try  to understand what the actual sizes of  the proteins are circular dichroism is a  another method that one can use to  understand the folded states of the  proteins and to compare batches of  proteins if you look in the top right  you see a spectrum of the green and blue  protein from different batches of  proteins that are slightly different and  similarly at the bottom right you see a  natural versus a recombinant  recombinantly expressed version of  protein and they have different spectra  and it's difficult to know exactly what  the difference is but it ultimately is a  different reflection of the ability of  the protein to absorb left and right  polarized light and the alpha helixes  the beta sheets etc given their  asymmetry will absorb right and  left-handed light in different manner  and if you compare batches of proteins  in time you will be able to see that  they're either the same or different  depending on their ability to rotate the  light using circular dichroism  Thermal shift assay is a method that one  can use to determine the stability of  proteins in different conditions and so  using a light cycler or a real-time PCR  instrument with fluorescently labeled  proteins one can apply a temperature  ramp and then measure the fluorescence  under different conditions so I show  that the top right proteins that are  melted under two different conditions  that gives slightly different midpoint  melting temperatures in the bottom right  I show the protein by itself or protein  with a ligand and no protein at all and  you can see that the ligand actually  causes the protein to be more stable and  therefore it takes a higher temperature  before you see the change in  fluorescence so this is a useful useful  for probing formulation conditions and  conditions for which your protein may be  more stable so with isothermal titration  calorimetry one can very rigorously  examine many different biophysical  characteristics of proteins so typically  one will incubate protein with with  another peptide or protein and then  measure the amount of heat that is  generated with small injections of the  peptide or small molecule and as the as  the peptide is injected heat will be  released and this heat and the sample  cell will be compared to a reference  cell and then ultimately with the amount  of heat absorbed one can obtain many  biophysical parameters associated with  the interactions of peptides and small  molecules with your protein of interest  so finally I show analytical  ultracentrifugation this tool is  is extremely rigorous it requires a bit  of a bit more training than some of the  other methods that it's able to answer  many questions associated with the shape  of the protein by itself or with  complexes how the protein behaves in  solution its purity its aggregation  state the association with other  proteins diameter mass the geometry etc  it's an extremely rigorous method and  the optima a you see is an offering from  beckman coulter that that provides this  in summary proteins represent important  therapeutic targets there are a variety  of tools I've just talked about some of  the more commonly used methods and today  in the future we made dive a bit deeper  and I welcome your feedback on some of  the methods that you think would be more  helpful to you and you biological  research obviously periodically  refreshing your protein analysis tool  box in your understanding of each of  these methods is critical for you to be  able to see and identify which tool is  most applicable to solving your for  problem of interest and moving onward  and accelerating the pace of your  discovery with that I thank you and I  welcome any questions thank you very  much thank you dr. Jones for that  informative presentation so it's time  for Q&amp;A if you have a question you'd  like to ask dr. Jones please do so now  just click on the green Q&amp;A button at  the lower left of the presentation  window type your question into the box  it appears on your screen and click on  the send button will answer as many of  your questions as we have time for and  while we're waiting for qu